hi there steve kaufman uh founder of  link here and uh i'm going to talk to  you about the third of my secrets to  successful language learning  uh the first one was to spend the time  every day and for as long as it takes  the second  secret was to do things that you like to  do in language learning and that will  ensure your success and ensure that you  stay with it  the third  secret is to  notice  what is happening in the language this  is extremely  important  now how do you develop the ability to  notice  there are a number of things that you  need to do first of all i would like to  stress that the ability to notice is  probably the largest difference  or the most significant difference  between people who are good at language  learning and people who are not good at  language learning so it's very important  to develop the ability to notice now the  first thing you need to do  is to  make sure you get a lot of exposure to  the language  through  listening reading or if you prefer  watching videos but somehow you have to  get a lot of the language into you  because you can't notice things that you  haven't already experienced  it's a bit like the  saying you can only learn what you  already know  it sounds a bit contradictory but  you can't notice until you've actually  consciously and subconsciously  experienced something so  hopefully you are  spending a lot of time  uh  gaining that exposure to the language  now how do you start noticing  there are a number of things that can  help you notice first of all you have to  want to notice so if i'm reading i'll  often underline certain  expressions or words if i'm reading at  link i will save words and phrases to my  personal database just the act of saving  them helps me notice now when i review  these in flashcards  that again helps me notice it's not that  it's not because i review them in  flashcards that i'm going to remember  them i won't necessarily remember them  some words i'll remember some i won't  but it's all slowly building up this  ability to notice  if i'm corrected  it may help me notice it may not but it  certainly won't necessarily correct me  maybe an explanation from a teacher or  an explanation in a grammar book will  help me notice and that's why i very  often uh review grammar books very  quickly  not again with the intention  or the hope that i'll remember a  particular rule or a particular verb  ending but because it's just that  process that continuous cumulative  process of getting me to notice certain  things and once i notice something  let's say it was a correction in my  writing and then i look for it when i'm  reading or when i hear the language and  all of a sudden i start to notice these  things everywhere and as we notice  things in the language they become a  part of us and pretty soon because we've  noticed it here and notice that there  suddenly automatically we start using  these words uh and phrases or patterns  of the language uh more correctly now  the same is true with pronunciation you  can't pronounce what you can't hear so  you have to pay attention to how the the  language is pronounced and it's not just  the individual sounds you need to pay  attention to it's the intonation and  sometimes it can be very useful to  listen and imitate because that's  helping you to notice so  whether it be pronunciation or correct  usage it's or the accumulation of words  and phrases it's very important to to  hone that ability to notice and it  starts with a desire to notice the  conscious determination will  to say i'm going to try to notice the  language and then you can train yourself  through these various activities  so that's the third hint learn to notice  the language and you'll become  an efficient and successful language  learner thank you
 

